  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Using generic VLMs (CLIP, SigLIP, Gemini Vision) for geometry diagram understanding. AG2's multimodal variant showed generic vision encoders produce 'marginal benefit via slightly different auxiliary point proposals' and 'image tokenization loses spatial information.' GeoGLIP (ICML 2025) found GPT-4o misperceives geometric entities in ~70% of cases. The field has decisively shown that natural-scene encoders cannot handle geometric structure.",
    "Evaluating only on text-dominant benchmarks where diagrams are decorative. ACL 2025's perturbation study showed models achieve near-identical performance without visual input on standard benchmarks, meaning claimed improvements may reflect text reasoning gains, not visual understanding. Always include vision-only/vision-dominant evaluation (MathVerse subsets, HC-M3D).",
    "Using sparse outcome-only rewards for RL training of perception or reasoning components. SGVR showed sub-goal rewards outperform outcome rewards by +37.5% SR; PRCO showed differentiated dual rewards reduce perception errors by 39.2% vs GRPO's 7.6%. Undifferentiated rewards cause perception to stagnate while reasoning improves (free-riding).",
    "Assuming continuous neural embeddings can interface with symbolic solvers. MAVIS, GeoGLIP, CogFlow, and CLIP-Math all produce continuous visual features that symbolic engines (DDAR, FormalGeo) cannot consume. The representation mismatch between neural perception and symbolic reasoning is the core bottleneck — only formal language output (CDL, DSL, predicates) bridges this gap.",
    "Building a benchmark without a solution method, or a method without a challenging benchmark. MathVerse and HC-M3D provide evaluation frameworks but not solution methods; many solver papers evaluate on text-dominant benchmarks that don't stress perception. The field rewards papers that advance both methodology and evaluation.",
    "Ignoring parser error propagation in symbolic pipelines. Geoparsing's 72.8% perfect parsing rate and DFE-GPS's 72.29% ConsCDL accuracy mean ~27-28% of inputs contain errors. Symbolic solvers are brittle to input errors — a single wrong predicate can invalidate an entire proof. DFE-GPS's perturbation-based robustness training is acknowledged as 'ad hoc'; principled error handling (verification-driven refinement, confidence-weighted predicates) is essential.",
    "Competing with AG2/InternGeometry on IMO problems where text suffices. AG2 solves 83% and InternGeometry solves 88% of IMO geometry problems using text-only input. These are well-defended positions with massive compute advantages (300M synthetic theorems, SKEST search). The winning strategy is targeting problems where diagrams carry essential non-textual information — a niche neither system addresses.",
    "Treating the CDL-to-DSL formal language bridge as trivial. Multiple papers (Geoparsing, ACL 2026 survey, DFE-GPS) identify this as an open engineering challenge. Different formal systems use different predicate vocabularies, different assumption handling, and different algebraic representations. A naive rule-based translator will break on edge cases; a learned translator (extending GeoLogic's approach) with verification-based validation is more robust."
  ],

  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions that will define the next 2-3 years of geometry AI research. First, **formal-language-mediated perception** is replacing continuous-embedding perception as the dominant paradigm for connecting vision to symbolic reasoning. The evidence is overwhelming: AG2's generic vision failure, GeoGLIP/MAVIS showing domain-specific encoders are necessary but insufficient (continuous embeddings still can't feed solvers), and Geoparsing/DFE-GPS demonstrating that formal language output is the only representation that bridges the perception-reasoning gap. Papers in 2025-2026 (RAPID, PRCO, CogFlow) still use natural-language captions as intermediates, but the geometry-specific instantiation of this paradigm demands formal language — expect CDL/DSL-based parsing to become standard by 2027. Second, **verification-driven training** is replacing outcome-driven training as the standard for geometry RL. SGVR's sub-goal rewards, InternGeometry's CBRL with DDAR verification, and GeoGen's step-level symbolic checking all demonstrate that symbolic verification provides qualitatively better training signals than answer-matching. The trajectory points toward end-to-end pipelines where every component (parser, translator, solver) receives verification-grounded feedback. Third, **modular neuro-symbolic architectures** with pluggable components are winning over monolithic end-to-end systems. Newclid's refactoring of AG1, RAPID's inference-time reasoner scaling, and the general perception-reasoning decoupling trend reflect a field learning that tight coupling prevents component-level improvement. The winning 2027 paper will likely be a modular pipeline where the parser, translator, and solver are independently upgradable, connected by formal language interfaces, and jointly optimized through verification-driven feedback — essentially a compiler architecture for geometric reasoning.",

  "meta_advice": "If I were advising a PhD student targeting a strong AAAI 2027 paper in this area, here is what I would say. **Pick the right fight.** Do not compete with AG2/InternGeometry on IMO problems — they have 300M+ synthetic examples and solve 83-88% already. Instead, own the niche of diagram-essential geometry: problems where the diagram carries information absent from or contradicting the text (MathVerse vision-only, HC-M3D paired-image tasks, solid geometry where spatial structure is inherently visual). This is where your dedicated parser provides irreplaceable value. **Solve the CDL-to-DSL bridge first** — this is your core engineering risk and your core intellectual contribution. The bridge between Geoparsing's CDL output and DDAR/FormalGeo's input DSL is not a 'boring plumbing' task; it requires careful handling of predicate vocabulary mismatches, implicit assumptions, and algebraic representation differences. Consider training a lightweight translator (extending GeoGen's GeoLogic approach) on synthetically generated CDL-DSL pairs from shared geometric configurations. **Use DDAR verification as your parser's training signal**, following SGVR's sub-goal reward pattern: decompose each parsed CDL predicate into a numerically verifiable sub-goal, use DDAR to check consistency, and feed verification success back as dense RLVR reward. This creates a virtuous cycle where better parsing enables harder proofs, which provide better training signal for the parser. **Ablate ruthlessly.** You must show: (1) dedicated parser > generic VLM (easy, given AG2's negative result), (2) DDAR2/FormalGeo > weak custom solver (compare with AutoGPS's DSR), (3) verification-driven refinement > single-pass parsing, and (4) your pipeline works specifically on diagram-essential problems where text-only systems fail. **Stay within compute budget.** HyperGNet trained in ~10 GPU-hours; InternGeometry used only 13K examples; Geoparsing's RLVR is lightweight. Your 200 H100-hour budget is generous for this type of work — spend it on the CDL-to-DSL translator training and the verification-driven parser refinement loop, not on scaling the base parser or solver. Finally, **frame your contribution as solving the representation mismatch problem**, not as building another pipeline. The PAR framework (ACL 2026 survey) identifies the Alignment stage — mapping perception output to solver-consumable representations — as the critical gap. Your paper's thesis should be: dedicated parsers producing formal language output solve the alignment problem that defeated AG2's generic vision, enabling symbolic verification to directly grade and refine perception quality. That framing connects your work to the field's deepest open question rather than positioning it as incremental engineering."
}
